;; std/ports.bjo — draining and streaming a textual port.
;;
;; The port vocabulary itself — `TextInputPort`, `TextOutputPort`, the `open-*`
;; family, `read-line`, `read-all`, `port->lines`, `write-line` and the rest —
;; is in `std/prelude`, which every program is linked against: opening a file
;; and reading a line from it are as much the language as `print` is, and a
;; header line is a poor price for them.
;;
;; What is left here is the part that takes the *reader procedure* as an
;; argument, and so is a library rather than a vocabulary: a program that only
;; wants lines never needs it, and one that reads records, headers or chunks
;; asks for it by name.
;;
;; The reader is any `(-> TextInputPort %a)` — `read-line` and `read-all` are
;; the stock two — and the element type follows it rather than being `string`
;; by fiat.
;;
;; The two *draining* readers are declared `-?->`, so they take a reader of
;; either colour and a bjoroutine draining a port suspends rather than parking.
;; The two *streaming* ones cannot be, and the reason is worth stating: a
;; sequence is emitted as a C# iterator, and an iterator may not await. Their
;; readers stay `(-> ...)` because that is the truth about where the value ends
;; up, not because the declaration was forgotten.

(export
  ;; Draining
  port->list port->vec
  ;; Streaming
  port->seq file->seq
  port->chan chan-next)

(import (std prelude))

;; `Exception` is only needed to spell `file->seq`'s `Result`. The alias is
;; module-local wherever it is written, so importing the class again here is
;; not a duplicate of the prelude's — it is the same name being made writable
;; in a second module.
(import/class
  (Exception (: System.Exception)))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Draining
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;;
;; Every remaining item, as a collection. `port->lines` in the prelude is the
;; `read-line` case, done in the runtime; these are for every other reader.
;;


;; The reader is `-?->`, so these drain at whichever colour it is given. A
;; `loop` lowers to a `while` in the enclosing method, and in the generated copy
;; that method is `async` — which is what lets `(reader p)` be awaited there.
(: port->list (-> (-?-> TextInputPort %a) TextInputPort (List %a)))
(defun (port->list reader p)
  (loop (:with i 1 1)
        (:break (port-eof? p))
        (:acc lst (listing (reader p)))))

(: port->vec (-> (-?-> TextInputPort %a) TextInputPort (Vec %a)))
(defun (port->vec reader p)
  (loop (:with i 1 1)
        (:break (port-eof? p))
        (:acc vec (vecing (reader p)))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Streaming
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

;; The items, pulled one at a time — the form for input too large to hold.
;;
;; The port stays the caller's: neither opened nor closed here, valid only
;; while the port is, and interleaving with any other read of the same port.
;;
;; **Good for one walk.** A second enumeration yields nothing.
;;
;; `:break` is checked before the yield, so the last line is produced and the
;; end of input is not read past; the `:with` is just a counter.
;;
;; The reader stays `(-> ...)`: this body is a `seql`, which is a `seq`, which
;; is a C# iterator — and `yield return` and `await` are mutually exclusive in
;; one member. Declaring `-?->` here would promise a suspending copy that cannot
;; be built, and the compiler would rightly refuse it. A stream of items read by
;; suspending work is a channel rather than a sequence, and that is `port->chan`
;; below — this one, with a rendezvous where the iterator is.
;;
;; So a bjoroutine calling this parks, and the blocking lint says so. That is
;; not a defect being tolerated: it is the honest report that this shape of
;; laziness is the one a C# iterator can express.
(: port->seq (-> (-> TextInputPort %a) TextInputPort (Seq %a)))
(defun (port->seq reader p)
  (seql (:with i 0 0)
        (:break (port-eof? p))
        (:yield (reader p))))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Streaming that suspends
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;;
;; The same shape as `port->seq` and the answer to what `port->seq` cannot do.
;; A sequence is a C# iterator, so it may not await; a channel is a rendezvous,
;; so it may. That is the whole difference, and it is why this exists rather
;; than `port->seq` being made to suspend.
;;
;; What a channel buys beyond suspending is that it is a *CML event*. A sequence
;; composes with nothing; a channel composes with `choose`, `timeout`,
;; `with-nack` and the rest — so reading a port with a deadline, or racing two
;; ports, is written here rather than arranged around the outside.
;;
;; The rendezvous is also the backpressure: a send waits until a receiver takes
;; the item, so a slow consumer stops the reader instead of growing a buffer
;; behind it.

;; A port, read on a fiber of its own, as a channel and the reader's promise.
;;
;; The promise is the end of the stream, because a `Chan` has no close. It
;; carries the failure too: a read that throws resolves it as `Err`, and the
;; consumer sees the exception rather than a stream that merely stopped. This is
;; the pair `async-seq->chan` hands back, for the same reason and in the same
;; shape.
;;
;; The reader is `-bjo->` and not `-?->`, which is the one declaration here
;; worth arguing about. It is called on a *spawned fiber*, so an ordinary reader
;; would park that fiber on every item — and `-?->` would let a caller in a
;; `defun` choose exactly that, silently, since an unconstrained colour defaults
;; to the one at the call site. A reader that runs on a fiber suspends; the
;; arrow says so. `(port->chan read-line p)` is still what you write: a name
;; with two copies has the suspending one here, because that is what this asks
;; for.
;;
;; This function itself is an ordinary `defun`. Spawning is colourless, so a
;; port stream can be started from `main`.
;;
;; The port stays the caller's: neither opened nor closed here.
(: port->chan (-> (-bjo-> TextInputPort %a) TextInputPort (Tuple (Chan %a) (Promise int))))
(defun (port->chan reader p)
  (let ((c (make-chan)))
    (Tuple c (bjo (port-fill c reader p)))))

;; The reader loop, which is what the fiber runs. Private: the pair above is the
;; interface, and a caller holding this would have a fiber's body without the
;; channel it fills.
;;
;; Answers the number of items read, so a consumer that only wants the count can
;; take it from the promise and ignore the channel.
(: port-fill (-bjo-> (Chan %a) (-bjo-> TextInputPort %a) TextInputPort int))
(defbjo (port-fill c reader p)
  (let go ((n 0))
    (if (port-eof? p)
        n
        (let ()
          ;; The send is the backpressure: this waits here until the consumer
          ;; takes the item, so the port is read no faster than it is drained.
          (ignore (sync (chan-send c (reader p))))
          (go (+ n 1))))))

;; The next item, or `None` when the reader has finished.
;;
;; The termination protocol in one place, because it is the part of a channel
;; stream that is easy to get wrong: a `Chan` has no close, so "no more items"
;; is the producer's promise resolving, and a consumer that only received would
;; wait forever at the end.
;;
;; The race is safe rather than merely unlikely, and the rendezvous is why. A
;; send completes only when a receiver has taken the item, so by the time the
;; promise resolves every item has already been handed over and there is nothing
;; left in flight for `choose` to miss.
;;
;; A failed read surfaces as `None` here and as `Err` on the promise, which the
;; caller still holds. Ending and failing look alike to a loop and different to
;; anyone who asks.
(: chan-next (-bjo-> (Chan %a) (Promise int) (Option %a)))
(defbjo (chan-next c done)
  (sync (choose (wrap (chan-recv c) (fun (x) (Some x)))
                (wrap (promise-join done) (fun (r) None)))))

;; The contents of a file, pulled through `reader` one item at a time and
;; closed for you. It can be re-enumerated.
;; Reach for `open-input-file` with `with-open` when the port is wanted for
;; anything beyond its contents.
(: file->seq (-> (-> TextInputPort %a) string (Result Exception (Seq %a))))
(defun (file->seq reader path)
  (match (open-input-file path)
    ((Ok probe)
     (let ()
         ;; First we just check if the file exists
        (close-input-port probe)
        (Ok (file-read/seq reader path))))
    ((Err e) (Err e))))
